@meta
id: 23-01
title: Let the buffer finish the word
chapter: 23
type: lesson
skills: complete-keyword
par: 76
minutes: 5
vscode: IntelliSense pops up as you type; you press Tab
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
`findByCustomer` checks the customer exists and then stops at a bare
`return`. Finish it:

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: customer.id });

Every identifier on that line already exists somewhere in this file. Vim
has no language server here, but it does have the file, and any word in
the buffer can be completed from its first few letters. Par is set so
that typing the line out in full goes over it; the point of this lesson
is to feel how cheap a long name becomes when you only type its start.

@keys
| `Ctrl-N` (insert mode) | complete the word from the buffer, searching forward |
| `Ctrl-N` again | next candidate |
| `Ctrl-P` | previous candidate |
| `Ctrl-Y` | accept the highlighted candidate and stay in insert mode |
| `Ctrl-E` | cancel, back to what you typed |

@hints
`/return$` puts you on the bare `return`. `A` to append, then type
` this.orderR` and press `Ctrl-N` while still in insert mode.
--
When only one word in the file starts with `orderR`, it is inserted
straight away. Keep typing: `.findByC` `Ctrl-N`, `({ customerI`
`Ctrl-N`, and so on. A key that is not part of the menu simply accepts
what is there and carries on.
--
`Esc` also accepts the highlighted candidate. So does `Ctrl-Y`. Only
`Ctrl-E` throws it away.

@solution
    /return$<Enter>
    A this.orderR<Ctrl-N>.findByC<Ctrl-N>({ customerI<Ctrl-N>: customer.id });<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Sixty-seven keystrokes. Typing the line in full is eighty-one, and the gap
grows with every long name a codebase has, which in a TypeScript
service is all of them.

How it commits: `Ctrl-N` inserts the first match immediately. If several
words match, a menu opens with the first one highlighted; `Ctrl-N` and
`Ctrl-P` move through it. Typing any ordinary character — a dot, a
bracket, a space — accepts the highlighted word and inserts the
character, so in the common case you never press anything to confirm.
`Esc` accepts and leaves insert mode. `Ctrl-Y` accepts and stays.
`Ctrl-E` is the only key that backs out.

Where the words come from: the current buffer first, scanning forward
from the cursor and wrapping, then other open buffers. That is why
`orderR` finds `orderRepository` rather than the `OrderRepository`
type: the property is used a few lines below the cursor and the type
is only at the top of the file. Note that your `ignorecase` setting
applies to completion too, so a lowercase prefix can match a
capitalised word when nothing closer fits.

There is a whole family behind `Ctrl-X`: `Ctrl-X Ctrl-L` for whole
lines and `Ctrl-X Ctrl-F` for filenames come next. `Ctrl-X Ctrl-O`,
omni completion, is the one that knows types and signatures — it needs
a language server, which is chapter 30.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: customerId } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: customerId } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId: customer.id });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}
